WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.285
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:01.952
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to DataVis DevTalk.

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:03.450
I'm your host, Minhaz Kazi.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.530
Our today's guest
is Mikael Thuneberg.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.480
Mikael is the founder
and CEO of Supermetrics.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.620
Supermetrics is a tool
that fetches marketing data

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.990
into Google Data Studio
and other platforms.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.330
Mikael, welcome to the show.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:20.770
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Thank you.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:21.680
Great to be here.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.580
MINHAZ KAZI: So Supermetrics
and Google Analytics

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.610
and its developer relations
team have a long history.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.370
And I've heard that there
was also a T-shirt involved

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:31.180
somewhere there.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.550
Can you tell our audience
about that entire story?

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:34.508
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Sure.

00:00:34.508 --> 00:00:37.570
Several years ago, I was working
at the marketing department

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.820
of a gaming company.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.920
And my job there was to do
reporting on our marketing

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.560
activities.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.700
But a large part of my time
went into copy-pasting numbers

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.920
from Google Analytics and
other sources into Excel.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.440
And that was really
time-consuming and boring.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.770
So when the Google
Analytics API came out,

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.100
I figured I could use that
to automate that process.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.620
And I did some googling
around the issue,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.940
and I found a forum post by
Nick Mihailovski, who is now

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.800
Data Studio PM, saying that
the first person who integrates

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.470
the Google Analytics
API with Excel

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:11.140
gets a free Google T-shirt.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.580
And I figured, that's perfect.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.270
I got to solve my own problem,
and I also got a free T-shirt.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.630
So I did a small script
that solves that issue.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:22.240
And I shared that with Nick,
and Nick said, that's good.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.250
And he sent me the T-shirt.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.620
But even more importantly,
he blogged about my solution.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.770
And that generated
a lot of interest

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.480
and led me to quit my job and
then to found Supermetrics.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.990
MINHAZ KAZI: That is amazing.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.950
In what areas does Supermetrics
help its customers, and how?

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:38.950
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Right.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.730
So marketers-- they
struggle with their data

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.400
being spread out in
various different systems.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.580
So they may be doing advertising
on Google, Facebook, Twitter,

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.860
LinkedIn.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.550
They have a web analytics
tool such as Google Analytics,

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.350
they have a CRM--

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.770
various other systems
where they have their data.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.740
And getting the full
picture is difficult.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.580
Many marketers, they still
rely on a manual process

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:04.970
of getting this data together.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.180
So they might be doing
copy-pasting into Excel

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.080
like I was doing.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.780
They may be downloading CSVs
and manually merging those

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.380
together.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.840
Or they may have hacked
together some scripts

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.260
that are unreliable.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.840
What we do is we automate
that process for them.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.200
So we have built
high-quality integrations

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.990
through all of these different
advertising and marketing

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:23.890
tools.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.310
And we get that data
together and put that data--

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.280
or make the data available into
tools that marketers generally

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.980
want to use, such as Google
[INAUDIBLE],, Google Data

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.758
Studio, Excel, and others.

00:02:34.758 --> 00:02:37.300
MINHAZ KAZI: How is Supermetrics
relevant for your customers?

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:38.717
You mentioned that
Supermetrics is

00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:42.140
pulling in data from all
of these different sources

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.350
and making it
available on the tools.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:48.100
So how are your users using
that data in Data Studio?

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.900
MIKAEL THUNEBERG:
Yes, so usually when

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.150
you advertise on
different channels,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.970
you want to see a
combined view of how

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.530
your campaigns are performing.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.520
So that's one thing they can
do to get a cross-platform view

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.870
of their campaign performance.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.420
They can also merge
their advertising data

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.590
with their sales data, which
is also an interesting use

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.380
case where they say,
OK, our advertising cost

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.170
this much and then [INAUDIBLE]
this much revenue based

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.400
on the leads we got.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.775
MINHAZ KAZI: And
you've also made

00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:19.150
a lot of templates
available for Data Studio

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.400
that people can use
with your connectors.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.320
MIKAEL THUNEBERG:
Yes, so what we

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.690
do when we create a
connector, we also

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.460
want to create a
high-quality template

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:28.970
to go with that connector.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.100
So it's really easy for
users to get started.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.000
So when you start using
any of our connectors,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.240
there's a premade template that
is populated with your data,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.445
so you immediately see
how your data looks like.

00:03:41.445 --> 00:03:42.820
Based on a template,
you can then

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.540
start doing customizations
and modifying

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.848
that fit your particular needs.

00:03:47.848 --> 00:03:49.390
MINHAZ KAZI: If an
organization wants

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.530
to make their own data available
to their customers for Data

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.640
Studio, how can Supermetrics
help them in that area?

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.860
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: So if there's
no connector already available

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.710
for that particular data
source, there are some options.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.020
We have something called
a custom [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.480
CSP XML connector,
so that you can

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.570
use to get data from any
endpoint that provides

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:12.650
data in one of
these three formats,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.280
so [INAUDIBLE], CSP, or XML.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:16.990
And our connector
takes that data

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.470
and processes it into the
format the data studio expects,

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.350
so it's really easy to get
that data into Data Studio

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.730
and start reporting
straightaway.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.640
That works in many
cases, not all of cases.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.160
So if that doesn't work
for you, the other option

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.140
is to contact us, and
we may be able to build

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.460
that connector for you.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.918
MINHAZ KAZI: For
Data Studio, we've

00:04:34.918 --> 00:04:36.610
seen a lot of users
and developers

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.400
who use it purely as a
marketing reporting tool.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.020
But there are also
other users who

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.980
are using it not just
as a marketing tool,

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:46.170
but also as a completely
full-fledged PI reporting

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:46.670
solution.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:47.628
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Yeah.

00:04:47.628 --> 00:04:49.540
MINHAZ KAZI: So what
are your views on this?

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.570
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Yes,
so I think right now

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.630
it's still largely used
for marketing purposes.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.550
And a large part of the
community connectors

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.200
are also marketing-related.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.790
Data Studios is a
great tool, and it

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.990
can be used for other
purposes as well.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.770
And I think that's
an area where we

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:09.170
are expanding to in the future
very, very, very strongly.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.010
So we are soon releasing our
first financial connector,

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.320
getting accounting
data into Data Studio.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.400
We are going to
follow up on that

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.160
with various other
kinds of connectors

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.660
that are not marketing-related.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.840
Accounting, finance,
HR, customer service,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.240
all kinds of different
choices that can

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.465
be visualized in Data Studio.

00:05:27.465 --> 00:05:29.590
MINHAZ KAZI: How should
small and medium businesses

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:31.900
approach their marketing
reporting and analytics

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:32.590
dashboards?

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:34.180
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Keep
it simple to start with.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.770
It's easy to get
a bit carried away

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.860
with all the cool features that
are available in Data Studio.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:42.610
You don't want to create a
cluttered dashboard with all

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.110
the different chart types.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.470
So I would concentrate on a few
key metrics to [INAUDIBLE] it,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.420
make a really, really
simple dashboard--

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:52.060
big metrics that the
audience understands

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.527
and that are actionable.

00:05:53.527 --> 00:05:55.360
MINHAZ KAZI: Two years
ago, we didn't really

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:58.510
have a big developer
community around Data Studio.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.300
Since then, we've launched a
community connectors feature,

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.640
we're working on
community visualizations,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.140
and more and more
people are getting

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:08.860
interested in building tools and
solutions around Data Studio.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.260
So what would your
advice to someone

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.135
who's just starting to build
solutions around this tool?

00:06:14.135 --> 00:06:16.510
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: I think Data
Studio is a great platform

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:17.650
for developers.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:19.600
It's really easy to get started.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.140
The documentation
is really good,

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.890
and you've done a
really good job on that.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:26.150
And also you've got a lot
of support from your team,

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:27.948
obviously, when you
build a connector.

00:06:27.948 --> 00:06:30.490
So getting started with building
connectors is really simple,

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.080
and I definitely recommend that.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.750
The challenges maybe
then come a bit later.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.430
Data Studio has a large
user base in the millions.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.850
So if your connector
really takes off,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.050
you may get a lot of users.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.010
And that brings new challenges
that you then need to tackle,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:49.000
such as how do you handle the
traffic flows with the rate

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.070
limits that are in
the data source APIs

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.430
that you connect to, so you
don't go over any limits that

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:55.690
are in the source APIs?

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.860
You need to do a lot of
caching in different places

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.280
to tackle that.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.230
You need to have
monitoring in place.

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.570
You need to be able to handle
all kinds of edge cases

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:07.480
with the queries, such as,
what if your query takes

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.490
over a minute to run when
the timeout for HTTP request

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:12.320
is 1 minute?

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:13.780
So all kinds of
things like this,

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.030
you need to then
start thinking about.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.930
But to get started,
it's really easy.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.850
MINHAZ KAZI: Mikael, thanks
again for being on the show.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:20.150
MIKAEL THUNEBERG: Thanks.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.340
Was great being here.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.110
MINHAZ KAZI: You can check out
the Supermetrics connectors

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:24.400
in the Data Studio gallery.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.180
Link is in the
description below.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.360
Make sure to subscribe to
the GCP YouTube channel.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.210
And you can always visit our
website at developers.googl

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.250
e.com/datastudio.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:36.590
Until next time, keep
building more Vis.

